<worksheet xml:id="hw-8-408">
  <title>Homework Assignment #8 - Math 408</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Due Sunday, 11/15</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      For this assignment, you will solve the problems given below with the following requirements.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each solution (with the possible exception of the portfolio report) consists of a clear statement of the theorem (or theorems) you are proving, followed by the proof of the theorem(s).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            You must write your assignment according to the writing criteria included with this assignment.  These should be consistent with what you learned in Math 210.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The last problem is a progress report for at least one of your portfolio problems.  Follow the instructions given for that problem.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      You may either submit a hard copy of your paper in class or submit your paper on Blackboard as a PDF file.  <alert>If you submit your paper electronically, it <em>must</em> be as a PDF file</alert>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <p>
    Your mission for this week is to prove most of the order axioms for our construction of the real numbers.  There are a few more challenging axioms that you will be working on for your portfolio project.  For all the problems below, you may assume that <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is an ordered field and that <m>\mathbb{R}</m> consists of all equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences of rational numbers under the relation we defined in class.  You <em>may not</em> assume Cauchy sequences converge.  Also, here is a theorem you proved on Homework Assignment #5 that may be helpful.
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-old-hw">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>\{ x_k \}</m> be a Cauchy sequence that does not converge to zero.  Then there exists an <m>N \in \mathbb{N}</m> and <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> such that for all <m>k &gt; N</m>, we have <m>|x_k| &gt; \epsilon</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  

  <p>
    Here are the properties you are proving for <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        ( pts.) Transitive Property: For all <m>X,Y,Z \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>X &lt; Y</m> and <m>Y &lt; Z</m>, then <m>X &lt; Z</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        ( pts.) Translation Invariance: For all <m>X,Y,Z \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>X &lt; Y</m>, then <m>X+Z &lt; Y+Z</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        ( pts.) Scaling:  For all <m>X,Y,Z \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>X &lt; Y</m> and <m>Z &gt; 0</m>, then <m>XZ &lt; YZ</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Work on one of the portfolio problems, and write a progress report on your work.  Your progress report will include one of the following.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              A statement of your theorem and a complete proof.  I will give constructive comments on your proof.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A partial proof along with both a description of the strategies you employed and specific questions that you want me to consider.  I will give constructive comments on your proof.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A description of the strategies you employed and specific questions that you want me to consider.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        Make your report clear and organized.  Take effort to carefully articulate any description of your strategies, and make your questions specific and carefully worded.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>